/Creator (Acrobat PDFMaker 17 for Word)
/Keywords (national security;commerce;environmental protection;governments;united states;department of defense;hispanics;international trade;economic development;money;standards;brazil;commodities;investments;security;data sets;economic policy;economics;literature surveys;new york)
/ModDate (D:20201008085034-07'00')
/Producer (Adobe PDF Library 15.0)
/Subject (Over the last decade there has been significant focus, particularly in national security circles, on the rise of China and its expansion in regions such as Latin America, Africa, and Asia. While it is relatively easy to identify literature examining Chinas rationale for expanding into Latin America, there is notably less that focuses on the reasons why Latin American countries let China in. This thesis seeks to understand why Latin American countries choose to economically cooperate with China through the form of accepting loans. By centering the agency of the countries receiving loans, this thesis identifies the economic and political decisions and institutions that have made Latin American countries potential partners for an emerging China. Overall, pre-existing commodity-based economic relationships and the presence of a centrist or left-wing government were the two factors most linked with accepting loans from China.)
/Title (Banking on Beijing: Latin American Economic Cooperation with China)
/Author (Carter, Patrick M.)
/Filename (/pdf/TRECMS/AD1126375.pdf)
>>
endobj
